Call What it does Returns <code>fork()</code> Creates an almost exact copy of the caller. Both continue from the line after <code>fork()</code> <code>0</code> in the child, child's PID in the parent, <code>-1</code> on error <code>exec*()</code> Replaces the current program's code and data with a new program. Never returns on success only on error <code>wait()</code> / <code>waitpid()</code> Parent blocks until a child exits child's PID <pre><code>int rc = fork();
if (rc == 0) {                 // child
    char *args[] = {"wc", "notes.txt", NULL};
    execvp(args[0], args);     // becomes "wc"
} else {                       // parent
    wait(NULL);                // wait for child
}
</code></pre> <p>Reading it line by line:</p> <ol> <li><code>fork()</code> splits the program in two. From here on, two copies run the same code.</li> <li>Each copy checks <code>rc</code>. The child sees <code>0</code>; the parent sees the child's process ID (a positive number).</li> <li>The child calls <code>execvp</code> to turn itself into the <code>wc</code> program (word count), with the argument <code>notes.txt</code>. Its old code is gone.</li> <li>The parent calls <code>wait</code> and sleeps until the child (now <code>wc</code>) finishes.</li> </ol> <p>This is exactly what your shell does every time you type a command.</p>
